---
title: Runtime Context
id: runtime-context
order: 2
description: "Pass typed runtime dependencies to TanStack AI tools and middleware without serializing them to the model or AG-UI protocol context."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - runtime context
  - typed context
  - tools context
  - middleware context
  - ag-ui context
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime context is application state you pass to tool implementations and middleware. Use it for request-scoped or client-local dependencies such as authenticated users, database clients, tenancy, feature flags, audit loggers, or browser services."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime context is not prompt context and is not the AG-UI "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunAgentInput.context"},{"type":"text","value":" field. It is never sent to the model automatically."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-type-safety-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How Type Safety Works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime context is checked from the point of view of the code that consumes it. Tools and middleware declare the context shape they need, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":", and framework hooks check that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" value you pass satisfies those requirements."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The source of truth is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition(...).server<TContext>(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" for server tools."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition(...).client<TContext>(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" for client tools."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatMiddleware<TContext>"},{"type":"text","value":" for middleware."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This means the context value is the implementation detail you provide at runtime, while tools and middleware are the contract. TanStack AI infers the required context from every typed tool and middleware in the call, merges those requirements, and checks your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" option against the result."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse, toolDefinition, type ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\ntype UserContext = {\n  userId: string;\n};\n\ntype TenantContext = {\n  tenantId: string;\n};\n\nconst currentUserTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"current_user\",\n  description: \"Read the current user\",\n}).server<UserContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  return { userId: ctx.context.userId };\n});\n\nconst tenantMiddleware: ChatMiddleware<TenantContext> = {\n  name: \"tenant\",\n  onStart(ctx) {\n    console.log(ctx.context.tenantId);\n  },\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [currentUserTool],\n    middleware: [tenantMiddleware],\n    context: {\n      userId: \"user_123\",\n      tenantId: \"tenant_456\",\n    },\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example, the tool requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UserContext"},{"type":"text","value":" and the middleware requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TenantContext"},{"type":"text","value":", so the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" value must satisfy both. If you remove "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tenantId"},{"type":"text","value":", TypeScript reports an error because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tenantMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" declared that it needs it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is intentional. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" object alone should not decide what tools and middleware are allowed to read. The consumers define their requirements, and the call site proves that it supplied them. Untyped tools and middleware still work; they receive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" context and do not force a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This inference also works when reusable tools or middleware are declared outside the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call and passed in as arrays. A consumer can opt into optional runtime context by declaring "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TContext | undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"; then the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" option can be omitted when all typed consumers accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":". If a context value is provided, it still has to satisfy every typed consumer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same rule applies on the client:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientRuntimeContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n};\n\nconst inspectClientContext = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"inspect_client_context\",\n  description: \"Inspect local browser context\",\n}).client<ClientRuntimeContext & { mode: \"debug\" }>((_input, ctx) => {\n  return {\n    tabId: ctx.context.currentTabId,\n    mode: ctx.context.mode,\n  };\n});\n\nuseChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [inspectClientContext],\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    mode: \"debug\",\n  },\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the client tool declares "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ClientRuntimeContext & { mode: \"debug\" }"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat()"},{"type":"text","value":" requires a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" value with both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"currentTabId"},{"type":"text","value":" and the literal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mode: \"debug\""},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-runtime-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Runtime Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define the context type once, use it in server tools and middleware, then pass the matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" value to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toolDefinition,\n  type ChatMiddleware,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { requireUser, db } from \"./auth\";\n\ntype AppContext = {\n  userId: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n  db: {\n    notes: {\n      findMany(args: { userId: string; tenantId: string }): Promise<Array<{ title: string }>>;\n    };\n  };\n};\n\nconst listNotes = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"list_notes\",\n  description: \"List notes for the current user\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({}),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ title: z.string() })),\n}).server<AppContext>(async (_input, ctx) => {\n  return ctx.context.db.notes.findMany({\n    userId: ctx.context.userId,\n    tenantId: ctx.context.tenantId,\n  });\n});\n\nconst auditMiddleware: ChatMiddleware<AppContext> = {\n  name: \"audit\",\n  onStart(ctx) {\n    console.log(\"chat started\", {\n      requestId: ctx.requestId,\n      userId: ctx.context.userId,\n      tenantId: ctx.context.tenantId,\n    });\n  },\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const user = await requireUser(request);\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [listNotes],\n    middleware: [auditMiddleware],\n    context: {\n      userId: user.id,\n      tenantId: user.tenantId,\n      db,\n    },\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When any tool or middleware in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call declares a concrete context type, TypeScript checks the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" value against that type. Existing untyped tools and middleware continue to work; their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.context"},{"type":"text","value":" type remains "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-runtime-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Runtime Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client runtime context is local to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" and framework hooks. It is passed to client tool implementations and is not serialized to the server."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChatClientOptions } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst notifyUser = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify_user\",\n  description: \"Show a notification in the current browser tab\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Updated tab ${ctx.context.currentTabId}`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [notifyUser],\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    toast: (message) => window.alert(message),\n  },\n});\n\nconst chat = useChat(chatOptions);","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use client context for local dependencies only. Do not put values there expecting the server to receive them."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-to-server-handoff","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-to-Server Handoff"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To send serializable client data to the server, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forwardedProps"},{"type":"text","value":", validate it in your route, and explicitly map it into the server runtime context."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst notifyUser = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify_user\",\n  description: \"Show a notification in the current browser tab\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Updated tab ${ctx.context.currentTabId}`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\n// Client\nuseChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [notifyUser],\n  forwardedProps: {\n    tenantId: \"tenant_456\",\n  },\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    toast: (message) => window.alert(message),\n  },\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"code","value":"// Server\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { requireUser } from \"./auth\";\nimport { serverTools } from \"./tools\";\n\ntype AppContext = {\n  userId: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const user = await requireUser(request);\n\n  const tenantId =\n    typeof params.forwardedProps.tenantId === \"string\"\n      ? params.forwardedProps.tenantId\n      : user.defaultTenantId;\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    tools: serverTools,\n    context: {\n      userId: user.id,\n      tenantId,\n    } satisfies AppContext,\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treat "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forwardedProps"},{"type":"text","value":" as client-controlled input. Validate and allowlist every field before using it to build server runtime context."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ag-ui-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AG-UI Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AG-UI also defines "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunAgentInput.context"},{"type":"text","value":", usually as protocol-level context entries for interoperable agents. TanStack AI surfaces that field through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chatParamsFromRequest"},{"type":"text","value":", but it is separate from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ context })"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI does not automatically copy AG-UI "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params.aguiContext"},{"type":"text","value":" into runtime context. If you want to use AG-UI context values, validate and map them yourself. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params.context"},{"type":"text","value":" is a deprecated alias of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params.aguiContext"},{"type":"text","value":" kept for backward compatibility."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { buildRuntimeContextFrom } from \"./context\";\nimport { serverTools } from \"./tools\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    tools: serverTools,\n    context: buildRuntimeContextFrom(params.aguiContext),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"}],"headings":[{"id":"how-type-safety-works","text":"How Type Safety Works","level":2},{"id":"server-runtime-context","text":"Server Runtime Context","level":2},{"id":"client-runtime-context","text":"Client Runtime Context","level":2},{"id":"client-to-server-handoff","text":"Client-to-Server Handoff","level":2},{"id":"ag-ui-context","text":"AG-UI Context","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nRuntime context is application state you pass to tool implementations and middleware. Use it for request-scoped or client-local dependencies such as authenticated users, database clients, tenancy, feature flags, audit loggers, or browser services.\n\nRuntime context is not prompt context and is not the AG-UI `RunAgentInput.context` field. It is never sent to the model automatically.\n\n## How Type Safety Works\n\nRuntime context is checked from the point of view of the code that consumes it. Tools and middleware declare the context shape they need, and `chat()`, `ChatClient`, and framework hooks check that the `context` value you pass satisfies those requirements.\n\nThe source of truth is:\n\n- `toolDefinition(...).server<TContext>(...)` for server tools.\n- `toolDefinition(...).client<TContext>(...)` for client tools.\n- `ChatMiddleware<TContext>` for middleware.\n\nThis means the context value is the implementation detail you provide at runtime, while tools and middleware are the contract. TanStack AI infers the required context from every typed tool and middleware in the call, merges those requirements, and checks your `context` option against the result.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse, toolDefinition, type ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\ntype UserContext = {\n  userId: string;\n};\n\ntype TenantContext = {\n  tenantId: string;\n};\n\nconst currentUserTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"current_user\",\n  description: \"Read the current user\",\n}).server<UserContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  return { userId: ctx.context.userId };\n});\n\nconst tenantMiddleware: ChatMiddleware<TenantContext> = {\n  name: \"tenant\",\n  onStart(ctx) {\n    console.log(ctx.context.tenantId);\n  },\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [currentUserTool],\n    middleware: [tenantMiddleware],\n    context: {\n      userId: \"user_123\",\n      tenantId: \"tenant_456\",\n    },\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the tool requires `UserContext` and the middleware requires `TenantContext`, so the `context` value must satisfy both. If you remove `tenantId`, TypeScript reports an error because `tenantMiddleware` declared that it needs it.\n\nThis is intentional. The `context` object alone should not decide what tools and middleware are allowed to read. The consumers define their requirements, and the call site proves that it supplied them. Untyped tools and middleware still work; they receive `unknown` context and do not force a `context` option.\n\nThis inference also works when reusable tools or middleware are declared outside the `chat()` call and passed in as arrays. A consumer can opt into optional runtime context by declaring `TContext | undefined`; then the `context` option can be omitted when all typed consumers accept `undefined`. If a context value is provided, it still has to satisfy every typed consumer.\n\nThe same rule applies on the client:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientRuntimeContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n};\n\nconst inspectClientContext = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"inspect_client_context\",\n  description: \"Inspect local browser context\",\n}).client<ClientRuntimeContext & { mode: \"debug\" }>((_input, ctx) => {\n  return {\n    tabId: ctx.context.currentTabId,\n    mode: ctx.context.mode,\n  };\n});\n\nuseChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [inspectClientContext],\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    mode: \"debug\",\n  },\n});\n```\n\nBecause the client tool declares `ClientRuntimeContext & { mode: \"debug\" }`, `useChat()` requires a `context` value with both `currentTabId` and the literal `mode: \"debug\"`.\n\n## Server Runtime Context\n\nDefine the context type once, use it in server tools and middleware, then pass the matching `context` value to `chat()`.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toolDefinition,\n  type ChatMiddleware,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { requireUser, db } from \"./auth\";\n\ntype AppContext = {\n  userId: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n  db: {\n    notes: {\n      findMany(args: { userId: string; tenantId: string }): Promise<Array<{ title: string }>>;\n    };\n  };\n};\n\nconst listNotes = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"list_notes\",\n  description: \"List notes for the current user\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({}),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ title: z.string() })),\n}).server<AppContext>(async (_input, ctx) => {\n  return ctx.context.db.notes.findMany({\n    userId: ctx.context.userId,\n    tenantId: ctx.context.tenantId,\n  });\n});\n\nconst auditMiddleware: ChatMiddleware<AppContext> = {\n  name: \"audit\",\n  onStart(ctx) {\n    console.log(\"chat started\", {\n      requestId: ctx.requestId,\n      userId: ctx.context.userId,\n      tenantId: ctx.context.tenantId,\n    });\n  },\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const user = await requireUser(request);\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [listNotes],\n    middleware: [auditMiddleware],\n    context: {\n      userId: user.id,\n      tenantId: user.tenantId,\n      db,\n    },\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nWhen any tool or middleware in a `chat()` call declares a concrete context type, TypeScript checks the `context` value against that type. Existing untyped tools and middleware continue to work; their `ctx.context` type remains `unknown`.\n\n## Client Runtime Context\n\nClient runtime context is local to `ChatClient` and framework hooks. It is passed to client tool implementations and is not serialized to the server.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createChatClientOptions } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst notifyUser = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify_user\",\n  description: \"Show a notification in the current browser tab\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Updated tab ${ctx.context.currentTabId}`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [notifyUser],\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    toast: (message) => window.alert(message),\n  },\n});\n\nconst chat = useChat(chatOptions);\n```\n\nUse client context for local dependencies only. Do not put values there expecting the server to receive them.\n\n## Client-to-Server Handoff\n\nTo send serializable client data to the server, use `forwardedProps`, validate it in your route, and explicitly map it into the server runtime context.\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst notifyUser = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify_user\",\n  description: \"Show a notification in the current browser tab\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Updated tab ${ctx.context.currentTabId}`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\n// Client\nuseChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [notifyUser],\n  forwardedProps: {\n    tenantId: \"tenant_456\",\n  },\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    toast: (message) => window.alert(message),\n  },\n});\n```\n\n```typescript\n// Server\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { requireUser } from \"./auth\";\nimport { serverTools } from \"./tools\";\n\ntype AppContext = {\n  userId: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const user = await requireUser(request);\n\n  const tenantId =\n    typeof params.forwardedProps.tenantId === \"string\"\n      ? params.forwardedProps.tenantId\n      : user.defaultTenantId;\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    tools: serverTools,\n    context: {\n      userId: user.id,\n      tenantId,\n    } satisfies AppContext,\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nTreat `forwardedProps` as client-controlled input. Validate and allowlist every field before using it to build server runtime context.\n\n## AG-UI Context\n\nAG-UI also defines `RunAgentInput.context`, usually as protocol-level context entries for interoperable agents. TanStack AI surfaces that field through `chatParamsFromRequest`, but it is separate from `chat({ context })`.\n\nTanStack AI does not automatically copy AG-UI `params.aguiContext` into runtime context. If you want to use AG-UI context values, validate and map them yourself. `params.context` is a deprecated alias of `params.aguiContext` kept for backward compatibility.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { buildRuntimeContextFrom } from \"./context\";\nimport { serverTools } from \"./tools\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    tools: serverTools,\n    context: buildRuntimeContextFrom(params.aguiContext),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Runtime Context\nid: runtime-context\norder: 2\ndescription: \"Pass typed runtime dependencies to TanStack AI tools and middleware without serializing them to the model or AG-UI protocol context.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - runtime context\n  - typed context\n  - tools context\n  - middleware context\n  - ag-ui context\n---\n\nRuntime context is application state you pass to tool implementations and middleware. Use it for request-scoped or client-local dependencies such as authenticated users, database clients, tenancy, feature flags, audit loggers, or browser services.\n\nRuntime context is not prompt context and is not the AG-UI `RunAgentInput.context` field. It is never sent to the model automatically.\n\n## How Type Safety Works\n\nRuntime context is checked from the point of view of the code that consumes it. Tools and middleware declare the context shape they need, and `chat()`, `ChatClient`, and framework hooks check that the `context` value you pass satisfies those requirements.\n\nThe source of truth is:\n\n- `toolDefinition(...).server<TContext>(...)` for server tools.\n- `toolDefinition(...).client<TContext>(...)` for client tools.\n- `ChatMiddleware<TContext>` for middleware.\n\nThis means the context value is the implementation detail you provide at runtime, while tools and middleware are the contract. TanStack AI infers the required context from every typed tool and middleware in the call, merges those requirements, and checks your `context` option against the result.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse, toolDefinition, type ChatMiddleware } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\ntype UserContext = {\n  userId: string;\n};\n\ntype TenantContext = {\n  tenantId: string;\n};\n\nconst currentUserTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"current_user\",\n  description: \"Read the current user\",\n}).server<UserContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  return { userId: ctx.context.userId };\n});\n\nconst tenantMiddleware: ChatMiddleware<TenantContext> = {\n  name: \"tenant\",\n  onStart(ctx) {\n    console.log(ctx.context.tenantId);\n  },\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [currentUserTool],\n    middleware: [tenantMiddleware],\n    context: {\n      userId: \"user_123\",\n      tenantId: \"tenant_456\",\n    },\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the tool requires `UserContext` and the middleware requires `TenantContext`, so the `context` value must satisfy both. If you remove `tenantId`, TypeScript reports an error because `tenantMiddleware` declared that it needs it.\n\nThis is intentional. The `context` object alone should not decide what tools and middleware are allowed to read. The consumers define their requirements, and the call site proves that it supplied them. Untyped tools and middleware still work; they receive `unknown` context and do not force a `context` option.\n\nThis inference also works when reusable tools or middleware are declared outside the `chat()` call and passed in as arrays. A consumer can opt into optional runtime context by declaring `TContext | undefined`; then the `context` option can be omitted when all typed consumers accept `undefined`. If a context value is provided, it still has to satisfy every typed consumer.\n\nThe same rule applies on the client:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientRuntimeContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n};\n\nconst inspectClientContext = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"inspect_client_context\",\n  description: \"Inspect local browser context\",\n}).client<ClientRuntimeContext & { mode: \"debug\" }>((_input, ctx) => {\n  return {\n    tabId: ctx.context.currentTabId,\n    mode: ctx.context.mode,\n  };\n});\n\nuseChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [inspectClientContext],\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    mode: \"debug\",\n  },\n});\n```\n\nBecause the client tool declares `ClientRuntimeContext & { mode: \"debug\" }`, `useChat()` requires a `context` value with both `currentTabId` and the literal `mode: \"debug\"`.\n\n## Server Runtime Context\n\nDefine the context type once, use it in server tools and middleware, then pass the matching `context` value to `chat()`.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toolDefinition,\n  type ChatMiddleware,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { requireUser, db } from \"./auth\";\n\ntype AppContext = {\n  userId: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n  db: {\n    notes: {\n      findMany(args: { userId: string; tenantId: string }): Promise<Array<{ title: string }>>;\n    };\n  };\n};\n\nconst listNotes = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"list_notes\",\n  description: \"List notes for the current user\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({}),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ title: z.string() })),\n}).server<AppContext>(async (_input, ctx) => {\n  return ctx.context.db.notes.findMany({\n    userId: ctx.context.userId,\n    tenantId: ctx.context.tenantId,\n  });\n});\n\nconst auditMiddleware: ChatMiddleware<AppContext> = {\n  name: \"audit\",\n  onStart(ctx) {\n    console.log(\"chat started\", {\n      requestId: ctx.requestId,\n      userId: ctx.context.userId,\n      tenantId: ctx.context.tenantId,\n    });\n  },\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n  const user = await requireUser(request);\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [listNotes],\n    middleware: [auditMiddleware],\n    context: {\n      userId: user.id,\n      tenantId: user.tenantId,\n      db,\n    },\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nWhen any tool or middleware in a `chat()` call declares a concrete context type, TypeScript checks the `context` value against that type. Existing untyped tools and middleware continue to work; their `ctx.context` type remains `unknown`.\n\n## Client Runtime Context\n\nClient runtime context is local to `ChatClient` and framework hooks. It is passed to client tool implementations and is not serialized to the server.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createChatClientOptions } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst notifyUser = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify_user\",\n  description: \"Show a notification in the current browser tab\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Updated tab ${ctx.context.currentTabId}`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [notifyUser],\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    toast: (message) => window.alert(message),\n  },\n});\n\nconst chat = useChat(chatOptions);\n```\n\nUse client context for local dependencies only. Do not put values there expecting the server to receive them.\n\n## Client-to-Server Handoff\n\nTo send serializable client data to the server, use `forwardedProps`, validate it in your route, and explicitly map it into the server runtime context.\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  currentTabId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst notifyUser = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify_user\",\n  description: \"Show a notification in the current browser tab\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Updated tab ${ctx.context.currentTabId}`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\n// Client\nuseChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [notifyUser],\n  forwardedProps: {\n    tenantId: \"tenant_456\",\n  },\n  context: {\n    currentTabId: \"settings\",\n    toast: (message) => window.alert(message),\n  },\n});\n```\n\n```typescript\n// Server\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { requireUser } from \"./auth\";\nimport { serverTools } from \"./tools\";\n\ntype AppContext = {\n  userId: string;\n  tenantId: string;\n};\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const user = await requireUser(request);\n\n  const tenantId =\n    typeof params.forwardedProps.tenantId === \"string\"\n      ? params.forwardedProps.tenantId\n      : user.defaultTenantId;\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    tools: serverTools,\n    context: {\n      userId: user.id,\n      tenantId,\n    } satisfies AppContext,\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\nTreat `forwardedProps` as client-controlled input. Validate and allowlist every field before using it to build server runtime context.\n\n## AG-UI Context\n\nAG-UI also defines `RunAgentInput.context`, usually as protocol-level context entries for interoperable agents. TanStack AI surfaces that field through `chatParamsFromRequest`, but it is separate from `chat({ context })`.\n\nTanStack AI does not automatically copy AG-UI `params.aguiContext` into runtime context. If you want to use AG-UI context values, validate and map them yourself. `params.context` is a deprecated alias of `params.aguiContext` kept for backward compatibility.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { buildRuntimeContextFrom } from \"./context\";\nimport { serverTools } from \"./tools\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    tools: serverTools,\n    context: buildRuntimeContextFrom(params.aguiContext),\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n"}
